      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The objective of this study was to project the health and economic impacts of providing a workplace smoking cessation benefit."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The authors conducted an update of a previously published outcomes model using recently published data and clinical trial results."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In four example workplace types evaluated, coverage of a cessation benefit resulted in greater numbers of successful cessations and decreased rates of smoking-related diseases. Total savings from benefit coverage (decreased healthcare and workplace costs) exceeded costs of the benefit within 4 years. Total savings per smoker ranged from 350 dollars to 582 dollars at 10 years and 1152 dollars to 1743 dollars at 20 years. Internal rate of return ranged from 39% to 60% at 10 years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Providing a workplace smoking cessation benefit results in substantial health and economic benefits with economic savings exceeding the cost of the benefit within a relatively short period."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CLINICAL SIGNIFICANCE",
          "content": "Providing a workplace smoking cessation benefit is projected to increase the rate of smoking cessation as well as decrease the incidence of smoking-related conditions and healthcare costs. In addition, workplace cessation benefits can result in decreased absenteeism, increased productivity, and net cost savings within 4 years."
        }
      ]},
